# Proximity Rules

## Scope

- BLE presence, badges, link sheet, host chain, invite queue
- Classic Bluetooth control channel, GATT fallback
- Session rules that decide LAN vs hotspot vs Wi-Fi Direct

What this spoke does not cover:

- Android hotspot/Wi-Fi Direct API calls → `android-wifi.mdc`
- Shizuku package detection → `shizuku.mdc`
- Existing mDNS service → `discovery.mdc`

## Invariants

1. Visible switch defaults on. Foreground: advertise and scan. Background sharing service: advertise and accept invites, do not scan.
2. Badges: Same LAN (`/hello` on a local subnet), Other LAN (address advertised but not that success), BLE only (no usable address and not on mDNS).
3. Untrusted non-LAN tap: link sheet, then the target sees nick, 6-digit code, host plan, link plan. Default host is the other phone. Trusted same-LAN opens immediately. Trusted different-LAN shows the sheet without the code. Trusted and neither on Wi-Fi skips the sheet and runs the host choice.
4. No LAN password (extract failed, or the person will not type): fall through to the private-network chain. Declining the short-code prompt or leaving the link sheet before a choice stops the attempt.
5. Host chain: chosen device starts a local-only hotspot. Android then tries Wi-Fi Direct if the hotspot fails. Then the next device already in the attempt. Desktops only try a hotspot. If a desktop must join and the hotspot failed, skip Wi-Fi Direct. All candidates failing ends the attempt.
6. Members may invite while the host setting is on (default on). Hotspot: the admitter and the new phone trust only each other, then the SSID and passphrase are forwarded. Wi-Fi Direct: only the group owner admits; a member relays. One invite dialog at a time; others wait; 60s timeout declines.
7. Control channel is classic Bluetooth, else BLE GATT. Frames are signed with Ed25519. Derived X25519 used to seal PSKs is not a second stored identity; its public value rides in the signed hello. No system Bluetooth pairing dialog and no Nearby Connections. Secrets ride only after Accept.
8. File bytes stay on the existing pinned HTTPS client, aimed at the address delivered on the control channel.
9. The visible switch does not keep an empty hotspot up. Idle default is 3 minutes after the last client leaves. Disband is manual and asks once. A transfer counts as in use.
10. Legacy BLE advert payload is 31 bytes. Advert carries flags, IPv4, port, short peer id, role, group id. Nick goes in the scan response. Never a fingerprint or a password.
11. iOS gets real platform code. Unit tests mock the channel. A simulator launch does not prove Bluetooth.

